{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## |Result 1|fork'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md | sed -n '94,245p'; rg -n '\"'^def (fork|stored|goldscore|probe)'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md | sed -n '59,72p'\"","aggregated_output":"3:## What the census and the traces show\n33:## Prompt audit (judging-v7 against the judgement ruler's reader brief and D260/D263)\n43:## The arms (revised after council A, `council-a-codex.md`; one lever each; every sentence sourced)\n57:## The probe and the decision rule (written before any J2 send; revised after council A)\n99:## What this cannot show\n104:## Out of scope for the council\n107:## Council rounds (Codex thread `01a1f7a7-e78a-7721-90a6-673bc85b6af6`, read-only, memories off)\n125:## Result 1: the final-request probe (2026-11-20, $1.21 spent of $3)\n148:## The fork probe (written before any send)\n151:  error. All 24 fork requests rebuild byte for byte.\n153:- **Metric (code).** A target \"repeats the rejected rung\" when its fork answer gives that rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats. Production\n156:  - **If v7 at the fork repeats the rejected rung on 12 or more of 24**, the errors are the judge's, not a sample carried forward. An\n157:    arm passes the screen when it repeats on at least 8 fewer targets than v7, with no more refusals. Its fork answers and v7's are then\n159:  - **If v7 at the fork repeats on 11 or fewer**, the errors are mostly one unlucky sample that the carried previous conclusion keeps.\n    94\t    dependants' consistency.\n    95\t- **The slice compares v7 and J2 on the same inputs.**\n    96\t  - Its arms are old reading with v7, old reading with J2, and c8 reading with J2.\n    97\t  - The first plan ran J2 in both arms; that is corrected in `PLAN.md`.\n    98\t\n    99\t## What this cannot show\n   100\t- **Opening, and the 17 matters never opened.** Phase 2.\n   101\t- **Effects across days.** A changed rung feeds the next day's previous conclusion. The end-to-end slice (plan step 7) shows those.\n   102\t- **A release verdict.** These cases are dev: they motivated the design. Acceptance is on a fresh window.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Out of scope for the council\n   105\tExtraction, opening, Steps 2–3, the frozen ruler's files, cost work.\n   106\t\n   107\t## Council rounds (Codex thread `01a1f7a7-e78a-7721-90a6-673bc85b6af6`, read-only, memories off)\n   108\t| Round | Verdict | What it found | What it taught |\n   109\t|---|---|---|---|\n   110\t| A (`council-a-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | J2a changed the order and the wording together; J2b's row 3 could bring back v5's stickiness, and its examples mirrored the failures; reading was not complete; the slice ran J2 in both arms | One lever per arm, literally. A blocking rule against \"unquantified\" moves conflicts with D263 behaviours of the frozen gold |\n   111\t| B (`council-b-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | The deck sampled same-rung answers. **J2a and v7 share an archive key** (`request_identity` sorts keys), so a populated archive replays v7. The loan-book example assumed one reporting convention. The rule was not paired | A change of key order alone is invisible to the archive's identity. Lock a paired rule by distinct question before any send |\n   112\t| C (`council-c-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | The refusal check read the stored final judgement as the previous one, giving false \"moved but changed is false\" flags. Coverage was not enforced | A check must run on the state before the answer it checks. Positive and negative controls: stored answers 0/48 refused, a cite mutant 48/48 |\n   113\t| D (`council-d-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | Invalid-rung answers fell out of the count. The replay preloaded production's whole day, which masked the theme queue | Start from production's own queue: 13 and 18 at the start, and the round adds 23 and 18, the same as old |\n   114\t| E (`council-e-codex.md`) | **CONSENSUS** | — | Send J2a and J2b (probe and gold, 3 repeats), score coverage and refusals, read the complete deck, then the production-path check under v7 and the passing arm |\n   115\t\n   116\t**Measured before the J2 sends ($0.60 spent):**\n   117\t- **v7 re-send, 48 cases × 3.**\n   118\t  - Targets: 21/24 reproduce the stored rung in all repeats; Q31 1→2 (3/3), old-2 Q16 1→3 (1/3), old-2 Q9 2→3 (3/3).\n   119\t  - Controls: 0/24 change. Refused: 0/144.\n   120\t- **v7 on the frozen gold, 51 × 3.** Agreement 45, 46 and 44 raw, 45, 46 and 42 strict (refused counted as not agreeing). Refused 3/153.\n   121\t  The stored scores are 15+15+16 = 46.\n   122\t- **Production-path replay, $0.** 72/72 requests are byte-identical to old's over 09-17 and 09-18, starting from production's queue,\n   123\t  with the same judged set.\n   124\t\n   125\t## Result 1: the final-request probe (2026-11-20, $1.21 spent of $3)\n   126\t| Arm | Targets moved off the stored rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats | Controls moved | Refused by the production check (of 144) |\n   127\t|---|---|---|---|\n   128\t| v7 re-send | 2 (Q31 1→2, old-2 Q9 2→3) | 0 | 0 |\n   129\t| J2a, order only | 2 (the same two) | 0 | 0 |\n   130\t| J2b, order plus check | 3 (the same two, plus old-2 Q16 1→3) | 0 | 0 |\n   131\t\n   132\t- **The registered falsifier fired at the rung level:** J2a moves no more targets than v7's re-send. J2b adds one.\n   133\t- **The derivations, read by Claude for 4 cases** (old-2 Q29, old-3 Q33, old-3 Q3, old-2 Q6), are unchanged under J2b:\n   134\t  - it still adds $25B and $18B;\n   135\t  - it still stacks the uplift;\n   136\t  - it still scales NVIDIA's $1.3T, now naming it a proxy.\n   137\t- **On M08 the material is ambiguous.** The items say \"$25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26\" and \"$18 billion\n   138\t  committed … for the remainder of FY2027\", with no stated relation. The readers' \"likely overlaps\" is an analyst's inference.\n   139\t- **The probe was confounded, and this was found before the slice, not at the end.**\n   140\t  - Every final request carries \"[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung N; reasons: …\", which is the rejected answer with its\n   141\t    whole derivation.\n   142\t  - The rejected rung had been held for a median of about 10 judgements: 1 to 46, and Q4 for 42.\n   143\t  - So the probe measures whether the judge overturns its own carried conclusion, not whether it would have chosen it.\n   144\t  - **The planned slice has the same flaw:** it starts from the 09-06 copy, with every earlier wrong rung carried in.\n   145\t- **The 432-card reading is deferred** (to be agreed). With the rungs unchanged, rule (a)'s floor of 12 needs the readers to support at\n   146\t  least 9 answers at a rung they rejected, on unchanged derivations. The reading cannot change the decision.\n   147\t\n   148\t## The fork probe (written before any send)\n   149\t- **Cases.** Per target, the stored judgement at which the rejected rung was first chosen and then held to the end. 6 are a question's\n   150\t  first judgement, with no previous conclusion. 18 are moves from another rung, where the previous conclusion is the rung before the\n   151\t  error. All 24 fork requests rebuild byte for byte.\n   152\t- **Arms.** v7, J2a and J2b, 3 repeats each: 216 calls, about $0.45.\n   153\t- **Metric (code).** A target \"repeats the rejected rung\" when its fork answer gives that rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats. Production\n   154\t  refusals are counted.\n   155\t- **Rule.**\n   156\t  - **If v7 at the fork repeats the rejected rung on 12 or more of 24**, the errors are the judge's, not a sample carried forward. An\n   157\t    arm passes the screen when it repeats on at least 8 fewer targets than v7, with no more refusals. Its fork answers and v7's are then\n   158\t    read blind by both families (216 cards plus plants) to confirm that the new rungs are supported.\n   159\t  - **If v7 at the fork repeats on 11 or fewer**, the errors are mostly one unlucky sample that the carried previous conclusion keeps.\n   160\t    The lever is then the anchor (how the previous conclusion is shown), not a reasoning check. J2a and J2b stop, and the anchor is\n   161\t    designed next.\n   162\t  - **If neither arm passes the screen while v7 repeats on 12 or more**, J2's levers do not fix these errors even without the anchor.\n   163\t    The J2 line stops and goes to Alex with this census.\n117:def stored(case):\n200:def probe(ledger_path, arm, repeats):\n322:def probe_card(case, answer):\n364:def goldscore(arm):\n581:def probescore(arms=('v7', 'J2a', 'J2b')):\n623:def forkcases():\n653:def forkprobe(ledger_path, arm, repeats):\n679:def forkscore(arms=('v7', 'J2a', 'J2b')):\n    59\t   - The stored final requests of the failing cases are re-sent with J2, 3 times each.\n    60\t   - As many supported answers are re-sent as controls, which must not regress.\n    61\t   - Blind readers mark them, mixed with the archived answers.\n    62\t   - **Pass:** the decision rule in `DESIGN-J2.md` (targets, controls and the frozen gold, each against v7's re-send).\n    63\t7. **A production-path check, then the end-to-end slice in week 1** (revised after council A).\n    64\t   - First, J2 and v7 run on identical inputs through the judge's own round, on a copy of one old store, over two days with updates.\n    65\t   - Then the merge-test harness (the 09-06 copy) is extended to every open question, 09-07..09-20, on two old draws. Its three arms are\n    66\t     old reading with v7, old reading with J2, and c8 reading with J2.\n    67\t   - Readers mark every answer, and the 32 matters are counted as dev.\n    68\t   - The cost is estimated from archived tokens before the send.\n    69\t8. **Acceptance** on a fresh window, run once on Alex's word.\n    70\t\n    71\t## Phase 2: opening\n    72\t- A census of why the 17 matters were never opened, after phase 1's end-to-end slice. It is not designed now.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
