{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"ls -la poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/goldprobe; rg -n '1,007|\\\\\"'$2.04|1007|whole window'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid --glob '*.md'\"","aggregated_output":"total 0\ndrwxr-xr-x@   4 lchingen  staff   128 Nov 19 22:47 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@  16 lchingen  staff   512 Nov 19 22:50 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 139 lchingen  staff  4448 Nov 19 22:51 J2a\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 155 lchingen  staff  4960 Nov 19 22:40 v7\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-f-brief.md:17:   a judge-only replay of the whole window from 07-01, with old's links fixed? That costs about $2 per draw and arm, from archived\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-f-brief.md:18:   tokens: 1,007 window calls price at $2.04 for two draws.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; rows=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); baseline={c[\\\"case\\\"]:p.stored(c)[0][\\\"rung\\\"] for c in rows}; sel={\\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q29\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q33\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q6\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q16\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q31\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q9\\\"};\nfor arm in (\\\"v7\\\",\\\"J2a\\\",\\\"J2b\\\"):\n cts=collections.Counter(); print(arm)\n for c in rows:\n  x=c[\\\"case\\\"]; ans=[json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"probe\\\"/arm/(\\\"{}-{}.json\\\".format(x,k))).read_text())[\\\"answer\\\"] for k in (1,2,3)]; repeat=sum(b[\\\"rung\\\"]==baseline[x] for b in ans); cts[(c[\\\"role\\\"],repeat>=2)]+=1\n  if x in sel:\n   print(x,[(b[\\\"rung\\\"],b[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"][:275]) for b in ans]);\n print(cts)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections,statistics,re; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; from hivemind.views import judging; rows=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"forkcases.json\\\").read_text()); cts=collections.Counter(); lengths=[]; sample={\\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q29\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q6\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q4\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q27\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q31\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q9\\\"};\nfor c in rows:\n s=c[\\\"case\\\"]; j,f,_,b=p.stored(c); vpath,apath=p.store_paths(c[\\\"draw\\\"]); v=p.readonly(vpath); a=p.readonly(apath); arc=a.execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where request_sha256=?\\\",(c[\\\"fork_request_sha256\\\"],)).fetchone(); u=json.loads(arc[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]; fu=f[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]; hist=list(v.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,rung from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id\\\",(c[\\\"view_id\\\"],))); after=[rr for rr in hist if rr[0]>=c[\\\"fork_judgement_id\\\"]]; assert len(after)==c[\\\"judgements_after\\\"] and {rr[1] for rr in after}=={j[\\\"rung\\\"]}; lengths.append(len(after)); prev=lambda q: next((line for line in q.splitlines() if line.startswith(\\\"[Previous conclusion\\\")),\\\"\\\"); pv=prev(fu); match=re.search(r\\\"rung (\\\\d+)\\\",pv); cts[\\\"final previous exists\\\"]+=bool(match); cts[\\\"final previous rung same\\\"]+=bool(match and int(match[1])==j[\\\"rung\\\"]); cts[\\\"no previous at fork\\\"]+=c[\\\"previous_rung\\\"] is None; x=judging.evidence_shown_in(u); y=judging.evidence_shown_in(fu); cts[\\\"different evidence ids\\\"]+=x\"'!=y; cts[\"theme blocks differ\"]+=u.partition(\"[Themes in force,\")[2].partition(\"[Previous conclusion\")[0]!=fu.partition(\"[Themes in force,\")[2].partition(\"[Previous conclusion\")[0];\n if s in sample:\n  print(s,\"fork\",c[\"fork_day\"],\"held incl\",len(after),\"final\",j[\"publication_day\"],\"evidence fork/final/shared\",len(x),len(y),len(x&y)); print(\"past FINAL\",pv[:405]); print(\"past FORK\",prev(u)[:405]); print(\"ORIGIN\",json.loads(arc[1])[\"implied_outcome\"][:300]);\nprint(cts,\"median\",statistics.median(lengths),\"min/max\",min(lengths),max(lengths)); print(\"lengths\",sorted(lengths))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; rows=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); baseline={c[\\\"case\\\"]:p.stored(c)[0][\\\"rung\\\"] for c in rows}; sel={\\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q29\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q33\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q6\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q16\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q31\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q9\\\"};\nfor arm in (\\\"v7\\\",\\\"J2a\\\",\\\"J2b\\\"):\n cts=collections.Counter(); print(arm)\n for c in rows:\n  x=c[\\\"case\\\"]; ans=[json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"probe\\\"/arm/(\\\"{}-{}.json\\\".format(x,k))).read_text())[\\\"answer\\\"] for k in (1,2,3)]; repeat=sum(b[\\\"rung\\\"]==baseline[x] for b in ans); cts[(c[\\\"role\\\"],repeat>=2)]+=1\n  if x in sel:\n   print(x,[(b[\\\"rung\\\"],b[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"][:275]) for b in ans]);\n print(cts)'\"","aggregated_output":"v7\nT-new-2-Q31 [(2, 'Below 1.5 GW by December 31, 2027; the disclosures do not support a tighter quantified estimate of eligible, non-duplicated capacity. The 2 GW figure is an upper target, while the quantified CDC and IREN project figures are not established as additional, fully DSX-eligible c'), (2, \"About 0.8 GW by December 31, 2027: start from the 72 MW NVIDIA collaboration and give discounted, partial credit to the 800 MW IREN Bundey development and CDC's 800 MW under construction, since neither full figure is established as Australian DSX capacity delivered or bindin\"), (2, \"Roughly 0.1GW of qualifying capacity by December 31, 2027, anchored by IREN's signed 72MW NVIDIA compute collaboration and rounded to about 0.1GW. The 800MW Bundey and CDC figures are not fully countable as DSX capacity under binding deployment contracts, and the other partn\")]\nT-old-2-Q16 [(1, 'About $25 billion or modestly above: carry NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace at an approximately $5 billion-plus annual run-rate, based on its reported trailing-12-month revenue. That puts the central estimate just over the $25 billio'), (1, \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This narrowly exceeds rung 1's $25 billion threshold, with some uncertainty because the Grace figu\"), (3, 'Approximately $20 billion in FY2027: the direct total-CPU outlook is the anchor. Grace’s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue is not added wholesale because it is historical and the management outlook is already stated as total CPU revenue.')]\nT-old-2-Q29 [(3, 'Approximately $54 billion at the FY2027 annual report: $25 billion reported as committed at July 26, plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced afterward, and risk-adjusted amounts for the reported $10 billion Anthr'), (3, 'About $58–62 billion at the FY2027 annual-report date: start from the disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027, then add the post-snapshot $3.5 billion MediaTek investment and a discounted contribution from the $10 billion An'), (3, 'Approximately $51 billion at the FY2027 annual report: the $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, plus the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced afterward, gives $46.5 billion; adding about $4.5 billion as a probability-we')]\nT-old-2-Q6 [(4, \"Approximately $1.19 trillion for the four largest: use the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy from the four hyperscalers' 2026 AI-infrastructure spending and the top-five 2026 capex base, then apply that share to NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate (e1786\"), (4, 'About $1.19 trillion for the four largest: apply the approximate $730 billion/$800 billion four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion top-five calendar-2027 estimate. This falls in rung 4.'), (4, 'About $1.19 trillion for the four largest hyperscalers: apply the approximate 2026 four-to-five share proxy ($730 billion / $800 billion) to the top-five 2027 estimate of $1.3 trillion. This is an approximate cross-measure proxy, not a directly stated four-company total.')]\nT-old-2-Q9 [(3, 'Approximately $384–389 billion: about $164 billion in the first half, roughly $100 billion in Q3 based on the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and an estimated $120–125 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.'), (3, 'About $385–390 billion: approximately $164 billion in the first half, roughly $100 billion in Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $120–125 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth and Rubin supply build.'), (3, \"Approximately $385–390 billion: about $164 billion in H1, around $100 billion in Q3 using NVIDIA's $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent Data Center mix, plus roughly $120–125 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth accelerates and Rubin supply builds.\")]\nT-old-3-Q3 [(1, 'About $446 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 using the 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance, and about $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, plus the previously derived $27–28 billion secon'), (1, 'About $445–446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue plus roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 using the $108 billion guide and the observed 3.1% average beat over guidance high ends, plus roughly $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth, gives about $41'), (1, 'Roughly $445 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, about $113.6 billion for Q3 using the $108 billion guide and the observed 3.1% average beat over guidance, and about $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth, total roughly $418 billion; the')]\nT-old-3-Q33 [(3, 'About $43 billion at the FY2027 filing, derived from the reported $25 billion commitment balance plus the $18 billion remainder-of-year commitment. The separate $42 billion of first-half equity spending is investment already made, not an additional outstanding commitment.'), (3, 'About $43 billion at the FY2027 annual filing, derived from the $25 billion reported as of July 26 plus the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027. This remains within rung 3 and below the $50 billion boundary for rung 4.'), (3, 'About $43 billion at the FY2027 filing: the reported $25 billion balance plus the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027. This is the best quantified bridge; the reported first-half investment spending and later deal amounts do not establish an additional outstanding b')]\nCounter({('control', True): 24, ('target', True): 22, ('target', False): 2})\nJ2a\nT-new-2-Q31 [(2, \"I estimate roughly 1.0 GW, and below 1.5 GW, deployed or covered by binding deployment contracts by December 31, 2027. This discounts the 2 GW combined target for the lack of partner-level allocations or deployment schedules; CDC's 800 MW under construction is a broader Aust\"), (1, 'About 0.4 GW of eligible capacity by December 31, 2027. This is a discounted forecast from the up-to-2-GW programme: CDC has 800 MW under construction across its broader Australia-and-New-Zealand portfolio, but the evidence does not establish what portion is Australian DSX c'), (2, \"Most likely about 0.8 GW by December 31, 2027 (roughly 0.5–1.0 GW). This forecasts the 72 MW IREN collaboration plus a discounted portion of the partners' larger development and construction pipeline; it does not count the full CDC or IREN portfolio figures as DSX capacity.\")]\nT-old-2-Q16 [(1, 'About $25 billion to slightly above it: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This puts the central estimate just over the rung 1 boundary, though the margin is narrow b'), (1, 'Approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera revenue plus a Grace CPU revenue run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. This carries the reported Grace trailing-12-month level forward and treats it as additional to the standalone Vera '), (1, 'About $25 billion or modestly more, implying rung 1. The FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion; Grace’s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion indicates a separate CPU business at roughly that annual scale, though the TTM period does not match FY2027 exactly.')]\nT-old-2-Q29 [(3, 'About $53 billion at the FY2027 annual report: start with $25 billion of commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027, add the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that disclosure, and risk-weight the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 '), (3, 'About $50 billion at the FY2027 annual report: carry forward the reported $25 billion commitment balance and the additional $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027, add the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment, then add only a modest risk-weighted amount for the undated $10'), (3, 'About $53 billion: NVIDIA’s $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives $43 billion; adding the $3.5 billion MediaTek convertible-bond investment and a risk-adjusted contribution from the reported Anthropic and Thinking Machine')]\nT-old-2-Q6 [(4, 'The implied figure is approximately $1.19 trillion for the four largest: apply the rough four-to-five share proxy of $730 billion/$800 billion (about 91%) to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate. The proxy mixes AI-infrastructure spending and broader CapEx, so it is appr'), (4, 'About $1.19 trillion for Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, and Alphabet: apply the approximate four-to-five share proxy of $730 billion / $800 billion (about 91%) to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate. This is an approximate cross-scope proxy, not a directly stated four-company'), (4, 'About $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion 2027 top-five estimate. The proxy compares four-company AI-infrastructure spending with top-five capital expenditures, so it is an approximation ')]\nT-old-2-Q9 [(3, 'About $384–389 billion: FY2027’s first half produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is implied at roughly $100 billion from NVIDIA’s $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale gro'), (3, 'Approximately $390–395 billion: the first two quarters contributed about $164 billion, Q3 implies about $100 billion from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and 92.5% Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $125–130 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Ru'), (3, 'About $385–390 billion: the first half delivered about $164 billion, Q3 implies roughly $100 billion from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is roughly $120–125 billion if growth reaccelerates as Vera Rubin supply builds. This carries the')]\nT-old-3-Q3 [(1, 'About $445–446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 after applying the reported average beat to the high end of the $108 billion guide, and about $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth, totals roughly $419 billion'), (1, 'About $440 billion. First-half revenue was $177.837 billion; applying the recent 3.1% average beat over the high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $127 billion. That is roughly $419 billion before S'), (1, 'About $438 billion: applying the previously derived roughly $27–28 billion SemiAnalysis upside only to second-half data-center compute to the $411 billion market estimate gives about $438–439 billion. The Q3 guide, first-half reported revenue and recent Q4 analyst outlook ar')]\nT-old-3-Q33 [(3, 'About $43 billion is the best supported estimate for the FY2027 filing: the disclosed $25 billion balance plus the $18 billion remainder-of-year commitment. The more recent evidence confirms very large investment activity but does not quantify how much of that commitment bal'), (3, 'About $43 billion is the best-supported estimate at the FY2027 filing: the reported $25 billion balance plus the $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year. The reported $42 billion of first-half equity purchases shows heavy deployment, but it is spending, not an '), (3, 'About $43 billion at the FY2027 filing, carrying forward the disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus the $18 billion planned for the rest of FY2027. The later evidence supports a high investment pace but does not establish a higher, non-overlapping outstanding commitme')]\nCounter({('control', True): 24, ('target', True): 22, ('target', False): 2})\nJ2b\nT-new-2-Q31 [(2, \"About 0.6 GW by December 31, 2027. I treat IREN's 800 MW Bundey campus as a DSX-aligned development opportunity, not as 800 MW already secured: discount it for execution and the limited time remaining, and use IREN's 132 MW AI-factory capacity—including its 72 MW NVIDIA coll\"), (2, \"About 0.7 GW by December 31, 2027, a forecast rather than a reported commitment: this discounts CDC's 800 MW under construction substantially because it covers a broader Australia-and-New-Zealand portfolio and is not all identified as DSX capacity. The other disclosures prov\"), (2, 'Roughly 0.8 GW by December 31, 2027, as a forecast rather than a disclosed contracted total. I discount the coalition-wide up-to-2-GW target because it covers more partners than the four named here and gives no allocation or schedule; CDC’s 800 MW under development is only a')]\nT-old-2-Q16 [(3, \"About $19–20 billion in FY2027, derived primarily from NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion total-CPU-revenue outlook and its standalone-sales clarification. I do not add Grace's greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue to that figure: the direct company wording is for total\"), (1, \"The most likely total is just over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion from standalone Vera CPU sales, plus Grace revenue at a current annual run-rate proxy of more than $5 billion. Grace's figure is trailing-12-month revenue, not an extra cumulative amount to add on top \"), (3, \"About $20 billion, near but not above the rung 3 ceiling. I treat the CFO's nearly-$20-billion total CPU figure as covering the standalone CPU category, rather than as $20 billion for Vera alone; Grace's greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue is therefore not adde\")]\nT-old-2-Q29 [(3, 'About $53 billion of commitments: the $25 billion reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives $43 billion; adding the later $3.5 billion MediaTek investment and roughly $6.5 billion as a risk-adjusted portion of the $10 billion Anthrop'), (3, 'About $53 billion: the $25 billion commitment balance reported at July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, then $3.5 billion for MediaTek announced after that remainder-of-year figure, plus roughly $6.5 billion as a risk-adjusted allowance against the '), (2, 'Approximately $35–40 billion of commitments at the FY2027 report. I start from the reported $25 billion balance as of July 26, do not add the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027 separately because it appears to overlap that contemporaneous balance, and add roughly $')]\nT-old-2-Q6 [(4, 'About $1.19 trillion for the four largest. Applying the approximate four-to-five share proxy of $730 billion/$800 billion (about 91%) to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate gives about $1.186 trillion; the proxy compares four-company AI-infrastructure spending with top-'), (4, \"The top-five 2027 estimate is $1.3 trillion (e5026). Applying the approximate four-to-five share proxy of $730 billion / $800 billion from 2026 (e1786) implies about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers; this is a proxy because e1786 measures four companies' AI-inf\"), (4, 'The implied estimate is about $1.19 trillion for the four hyperscalers. I apply the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion 2027 top-five estimate; the proxy compares four-company AI-infrastructure spending with top-five capital expenditures, so it ')]\nT-old-2-Q9 [(1, 'NVIDIA reported about $164 billion in the first half; subtracting that from the $414 billion reference gives roughly $250 billion of reference H2 revenue. Applying SemiAnalysis’s forecast that H2 Data Center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% implies about $300 bil'), (3, 'About $390–395 billion: the first two quarters total approximately $164 billion; applying the latest Data Center mix to NVIDIA’s $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100 billion for Q3. I estimate roughly $126–131 billion for Q4, allowing for the stated hypersc'), (3, 'About $384–389 billion: the first two quarters contributed roughly $164 billion; Q3 is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix; and Q4 is approximately $120–125 billion as growth reaccelerates with Rubin supply. The $120–125 b')]\nT-old-3-Q3 [(1, 'About $446 billion: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; applying the reported 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance to the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the 12% sequential growth pace forward implies about $127 billi'), (1, 'About $445–446 billion, rung 1. The run-rate calculation is $177.837 billion for the first half, about $113.6 billion for Q3 using the $108 billion midpoint guide and the observed 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance, and about $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward'), (1, 'About $446 billion: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; applying the observed 3.1% average beat over the high end of Q3 guidance implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the 12% sequential growth pace forward implies about $127 billion for Q4. The previously ')]\nT-old-3-Q33 [(3, 'The evidence still implies roughly $43 billion at the FY2027 filing: $25 billion reported as commitments plus the $18 billion earmarked for the rest of FY2027. I do not add reported equity investments or later deal announcements on top: the evidence does not establish that t'), (3, 'The disclosed company figures imply roughly $43 billion of commitments before accounting for commitments fulfilled by the annual filing. The reported investment spending and subsequent deal announcements do not quantify an additional outstanding balance at that date.'), (3, 'About $43 billion is the best-supported proxy for the FY2027 filing: the disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus the $18 billion remainder-of-year commitment envelope. I do not add the named investments separately because the evidence does not show they fall outside th')]\nCounter({('control', True): 24, ('target', True): 21, ('target', False): 3})\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections,statistics,re; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; from hivemind.views import judging; rows=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"forkcases.json\\\").read_text()); cts=collections.Counter(); lengths=[]; sample={\\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q29\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q6\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q4\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q27\\\",\\\"T-new-2-Q31\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q9\\\"};\nfor c in rows:\n s=c[\\\"case\\\"]; j,f,_,b=p.stored(c); vpath,apath=p.store_paths(c[\\\"draw\\\"]); v=p.readonly(vpath); a=p.readonly(apath); arc=a.execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where request_sha256=?\\\",(c[\\\"fork_request_sha256\\\"],)).fetchone(); u=json.loads(arc[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]; fu=f[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]; hist=list(v.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,rung from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id\\\",(c[\\\"view_id\\\"],))); after=[rr for rr in hist if rr[0]>=c[\\\"fork_judgement_id\\\"]]; assert len(after)==c[\\\"judgements_after\\\"] and {rr[1] for rr in after}=={j[\\\"rung\\\"]}; lengths.append(len(after)); prev=lambda q: next((line for line in q.splitlines() if line.startswith(\\\"[Previous conclusion\\\")),\\\"\\\"); pv=prev(fu); match=re.search(r\\\"rung (\\\\d+)\\\",pv); cts[\\\"final previous exists\\\"]+=bool(match); cts[\\\"final previous rung same\\\"]+=bool(match and int(match[1])==j[\\\"rung\\\"]); cts[\\\"no previous at fork\\\"]+=c[\\\"previous_rung\\\"] is None; x=judging.evidence_shown_in(u); y=judging.evidence_shown_in(fu); cts[\\\"different evidence ids\\\"]+=x\"'!=y; cts[\"theme blocks differ\"]+=u.partition(\"[Themes in force,\")[2].partition(\"[Previous conclusion\")[0]!=fu.partition(\"[Themes in force,\")[2].partition(\"[Previous conclusion\")[0];\n if s in sample:\n  print(s,\"fork\",c[\"fork_day\"],\"held incl\",len(after),\"final\",j[\"publication_day\"],\"evidence fork/final/shared\",len(x),len(y),len(x&y)); print(\"past FINAL\",pv[:405]); print(\"past FORK\",prev(u)[:405]); print(\"ORIGIN\",json.loads(arc[1])[\"implied_outcome\"][:300]);\nprint(cts,\"median\",statistics.median(lengths),\"min/max\",min(lengths),max(lengths)); print(\"lengths\",sorted(lengths))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"T-new-2-Q27 fork 2026-08-27 held incl 1 final 2026-08-27 evidence fork/final/shared 1 1 1\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\npast FORK [Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\nORIGIN Rung 3, Still open, is the most likely outcome by January 31, 2027: the only item reports consideration, not a decision or schedule, and the White House statement is not a formal abandonment. No tariff rate or effective date can be inferred.\nT-new-2-Q31 fork 2026-09-16 held incl 1 final 2026-09-16 evidence fork/final/shared 30 30 30\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's announced target of “up to 2 gigawatts” by 2027, which sits in rung 3. That is an upper target, not a disclosed amount already covered by binding deployment contracts. The evidence supports some real pipeline and deployment, but not enough eligible, non-duplicated DSX capacity to reach the 1.5 GW boundary: CDC's 800 M\npast FORK [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's announced target of “up to 2 gigawatts” by 2027, which sits in rung 3. That is an upper target, not a disclosed amount already covered by binding deployment contracts. The evidence supports some real pipeline and deployment, but not enough eligible, non-duplicated DSX capacity to reach the 1.5 GW boundary: CDC's 800 M\nORIGIN Roughly 0.3 GW by December 31, 2027: 72 MW is the clearest quantified NVIDIA collaboration, with a modest allowance for further deployment from the broader programme, but the unsized and unscheduled balance of the up-to-2-GW target is discounted. The estimate is below 0.5 GW, so it falls in rung 1.\nT-new-2-Q4 fork 2026-07-02 held incl 42 final 2026-09-20 evidence fork/final/shared 10 43 3\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The market reference is rung 3 (Still open): no consensus, current Commerce/BIS plan, or deadline for a qualifying announcement is supplied. New evidence raises the likelihood of rung 1: Commerce is reported to be formally investigating chip access, the White House has accused Moonshot of accessing Blackwell servers in Thailand, and Taiwan pro\npast FORK [Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\nORIGIN Most likely, the matter remains open on January 31, 2027, with neither a qualifying Commerce/BIS action nor formal abandonment. The evidence identifies an existing probe and related Singapore proceedings, but gives no U.S. finding, action, or abandonment date to carry into the post-July 2 window.\nT-old-2-Q29 fork 2026-09-15 held incl 6 final 2026-09-20 evidence fork/final/shared 44 35 27\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is Bank of America’s roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA’s equity investments in ecosystem partners, which sits at rung 3; its period and whether it measures outstanding commitments are unclear. The company’s $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives a $43 billion base. Adding \npast FORK [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Bank of America's approximately $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments, which sits at rung 3, but its period and whether it measures future commitments outstanding at the FY2027 annual report are unclear. The more date-relevant company figures imply a $43 billion baseline: $25 billion of commitments as of July 26 \nORIGIN About $51.5 billion at the FY2027 annual-report date: $25 billion reported as committed as of July 26, plus $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year and the separately announced $3.5 billion MediaTek investment, with roughly $5 billion risk-adjusted from the reported potential Anthropic\nT-old-2-Q6 fork 2026-08-26 held incl 20 final 2026-09-19 evidence fork/final/shared 23 31 10\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The market reference is the $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate. That clears rung 4’s $1.1 trillion floor but remains below rung 5’s $1.2\npast FORK [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is Bank of America's reported calendar-2027 consensus of $1.08 trillion, which sits in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion). The current forecast remains around $1.05 trillion: UBS's 25% 2027 growth applied to the prior judgment's roughly $864 billion 2026 base implies about $1.08 trillion, while the newer rolling consens\nORIGIN About $1.19 trillion for the four largest in calendar 2027: scale NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast by the approximate four-to-five-company ratio in the disclosed 2026 figures ($730 billion / $800 billion, or about 91%). This is a rough scope adjustment because the cited figures do not use pe\nT-old-2-Q9 fork 2026-09-20 held incl 1 final 2026-09-20 evidence fork/final/shared 123 123 123\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The current evidence implies about $385–390 billion: the first two quarters produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–12\npast FORK [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The current evidence implies about $385–390 billion: the first two quarters produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–12\nORIGIN Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin r\nT-old-3-Q3 fork 2026-09-11 held incl 10 final 2026-09-20 evidence fork/final/shared 232 200 26\npast FINAL [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The market reference is about $411 billion (e5406), which is rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate remains above the rung-1 boundary: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion (e2754); applying the observed 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance to the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 (e2715, e5088, e5089); carr\npast FORK [Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The market reference is the latest Wall Street estimate of about $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The reported first-half total of $177.837 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and carrying the guided roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply about $406.8 billion—below the reference but still above rung 2's $392 billion floor and b\nORIGIN About $435 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus $108 billion Q3 guidance and a 12% sequential Q4 carry imply about $406.8 billion before incremental upside. SemiAnalysis’s 20% above-consensus forecast is applied only to a conservatively discounted share of second-half revenue, rath\nCounter({'final previous exists': 23, 'final previous rung same': 21, 'different evidence ids': 18, 'theme blocks differ': 14, 'no previous at fork': 6}) median 8.5 min/max 1 46\nlengths [1, 1, 1, 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9, 10, 10, 11, 17, 19, 20, 22, 24, 42, 45, 46]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib; root=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/probe\\\"); names=[\\\"T-new-2-Q20\\\",\\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q37\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q41\\\",\\\"T-old-1-Q28\\\"];\nfor n in names:\n print(n)\n for arm in (\\\"v7\\\",\\\"J2a\\\",\\\"J2b\\\"):\n  for k in (1,2,3):\n   x=json.loads((root/arm/(\\\"{}-{}.json\\\".format(n,k))).read_text())[\\\"answer\\\"]; print(arm,k,x[\\\"rung\\\"],x[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"][:500])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib; root=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/probe\\\"); names=[\\\"T-new-2-Q20\\\",\\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q37\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q41\\\",\\\"T-old-1-Q28\\\"];\nfor n in names:\n print(n)\n for arm in (\\\"v7\\\",\\\"J2a\\\",\\\"J2b\\\"):\n  for k in (1,2,3):\n   x=json.loads((root/arm/(\\\"{}-{}.json\\\".format(n,k))).read_text())[\\\"answer\\\"]; print(arm,k,x[\\\"rung\\\"],x[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"][:500])'\"","aggregated_output":"T-new-2-Q20\nv7 1 3 Roughly 28%: carry forward the prior estimate based on S&P’s reported $225 billion issued through July and its projection of $400 billion by year-end, calibrated against the reported up-to-30% share in some IG indexes. The evidence does not establish that all of the broader segment tally is AI-infrastructure borrowing.\nv7 2 3 Roughly 28%, retaining the broad S&P segment tally's year-end projection as the best full-year indicator; no new evidence gives a quantified change to that estimate or a replacement denominator.\nv7 3 3 Roughly 28% for calendar 2026, retaining the prior broader-market estimate based on the S&P-related segment’s $225 billion issued by end-July and its projection of $400 billion by year-end compared with net new investment-grade issuance. The latest evidence does not quantify a change to that projection or denominator.\nJ2a 1 3 About 28% for 2026, retaining the prior conclusion’s conversion of S&P Global’s approximately $225 billion issued by the end of July and its $400 billion year-end projection into a share of net new investment-grade issuance (e2360). The later NVIDIA $25 billion issue is not added again: it is within the broader hyperscaler-and-related-company tally, and its stated use was for general corporate purposes (e2806).\nJ2a 2 3 About 28%, remaining in rung 3. The S&P-reported $225 billion issued through July and potential $400 billion by year-end support the broader segment issuance trajectory; with no new denominator or quantified revision in the evidence shown, the prior implied share carries forward.\nJ2a 3 3 Approximately 28% for calendar 2026. The broader S&P-related issuance tally of about $225 billion through July and its projection of $400 billion by year-end still imply roughly 28% against the market-wide denominator carried from the prior in-force calculation; NVIDIA’s $25 billion issue is not added separately to that broader tally.\nJ2b 1 3 About 28%, remaining in rung 3. The S&P-reported $225 billion issued by hyperscalers and related companies through July, with a possible $400 billion by year-end, supports a high share, but this broader segment is only a proxy for AI-infrastructure borrowers and does not supply a directly calculable full-market net-new share. NVIDIA’s $25 billion issue is not added separately: it was issued by June and may already be within the S&P tally; its stated use was general corporate purposes.\nJ2b 2 3 Roughly 28% for calendar 2026, carrying forward the prior estimate from the broader S&P segment tally and year-end projection. The reported $400 billion projection is for hyperscalers and related companies’ bond issuance, not specifically AI-infrastructure borrowers’ net new investment-grade issuance, so it cannot be used directly as the answer or to recompute the share without a matching denominator.\nJ2b 3 3 About 28% for the broad full-year measure, retaining the prior broad-segment estimate: S&P’s reported $225 billion issued through July and possible $400 billion by year-end support a high share, while the reported 30% applies only to some indexes. The evidence shown does not supply the denominator needed to independently calculate an exact broad-market percentage, so $400 billion is treated as a segment proxy rather than as the answer itself.\nT-new-3-Q26\nv7 1 2 About $24.9bn: Poolside $7bn, Groq $17bn from $13bn at closing plus $4bn payable, and Enfabrica about $0.9bn. Groq's reported cash outflow and accrued consideration are included within, not added to, the Groq amount.\nv7 2 2 About $24.9bn: Poolside $7bn ($6bn licence plus $1bn investment), Groq $17bn ($13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable), and Enfabrica about $0.9bn. Groq payment and accrual figures are included within that arrangement, not added again.\nv7 3 2 About $24.9bn by FY2027 year-end: $7bn for Poolside, $17bn for Groq based on $13bn paid at closing plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. The Groq payment and accrual disclosures are counted within that $17bn, not added again.\nJ2a 1 2 The evidence implies about $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn closing consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. Groq's reported $2.944bn cash outflow and $986m accrued consideration are payment/accounting details within the arrangement, not additional amounts to add.\nJ2a 2 2 About $24.9bn: Poolside’s $6bn licence plus its $1bn investment commitment, Groq’s disclosed $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. The reported Groq cash outflow and accrued amount are payment details within that arrangement, not additional consideration.\nJ2a 3 2 About $24.9bn by FY2027 year-end: Poolside’s $6bn licence plus $1bn investment, Groq’s disclosed $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. I count the Poolside investment as committed on the announced terms and do not add Groq payment details again.\nJ2b 1 2 About $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus the announced $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. I count the Groq cash outflow and accrued consideration as payment details within its arrangement, not as additional amounts.\nJ2b 2 2 About $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus the announced $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. The Groq cash outflow and accrued consideration are payment details within that arrangement, not additional amounts to add.\nJ2b 3 2 About $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. I count the Groq cash outflow and accrued consideration as payment details within its arrangement, not as amounts to add again.\nT-old-3-Q37\nv7 1 3 About 72.0% for FY2028: use the bottom of management’s 72%–73% outlook because rising memory costs and tight supply pressure margins, while the negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery. No supplied evidence quantifies an NVIDIA-specific cost impact large enough to take the forecast below the guidance floor.\nv7 2 3 About 72.0%: use the lower end of management’s FY2028 guided range because executed price increases support recovery, while rising memory costs and tight supply weigh against a result above the floor. This is rung 3 and does not cross the >72% boundary into rung 2.\nv7 3 3 About 72.0%, at the guidance range’s lower edge. Executed price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery, but rising memory costs and persistent supply tightness favor the low end of management’s range.\nJ2a 1 3 About 72.0%, at the low end of guidance: negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery, while worsening memory costs and tight supply weigh against a result above the guidance floor.\nJ2a 2 3 About 72.0%. The guidance range anchors the estimate, while rising memory costs and tight supply bias the result toward its 72% floor; negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery but do not justify assuming a result above the floor.\nJ2a 3 3 About 72.0%, at the lower end of guidance: the forecast recovery from negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 FY2028 is offset by further memory-cost increases and evidence that supply will remain constrained.\nJ2b 1 3 About 72%. The negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 FY2028 support recovery, but memory prices are expected to rise further and supply is described as tight; with no quantified NVIDIA-specific cost impact to adjust the guidance by, the evidence points to its lower endpoint.\nJ2b 2 3 About 72.0%. I use the FY2028 guidance as the anchor, with the rising memory costs and tight supply making the bottom of the range more likely; negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery to that floor.\nJ2b 3 3 About 72.0%. The guidance remains in force, and negotiated price increases support recovery; however, higher-than-expected memory costs and evidence of persistent tight supply make the guidance floor more likely than a result above 72%.\nT-old-3-Q41\nv7 1 3 Roughly $38–39 billion: discount the management expectation of more than $40 billion for the absence of Vera shipment volume in hand, with AMD’s reported performance comparisons adding execution and competitiveness risk.\nv7 2 3 About $39 billion for FY2028: the management expectation points above $40 billion, but I discount it because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand and AMD’s reported performance results raise execution risk. This is below $40 billion and within rung 3.\nv7 3 3 About $37–38 billion: I use management’s more-than-$40-billion implication as the starting point, discount it because Vera shipments in hand are not shown, and lower the estimate modestly for the new AMD performance comparisons. This is at least $30 billion but less than $40 billion, or rung 3.\nJ2a 1 3 Approximately $38–39 billion. The management expectation sets a greater-than-$40 billion baseline; I discount it because the evidence gives no Vera shipment volume in hand, and the specific AMD performance comparisons increase execution and competitiveness risk.\nJ2a 2 3 Approximately $38–39 billion of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue. I start from management's more-than-$40-billion expectation, but discount it because the evidence does not establish Vera shipment volume in hand; the deployment and evaluation statements are not sized, and the reported competitive benchmarks add execution risk.\nJ2a 3 3 I imply approximately $38–39 billion for Vera CPU revenue: I discount the more-than-$40-billion management target because it is a target rather than volume shown in hand, and the AMD-reported performance comparisons add competitive risk. The demand and deployment evidence supports revenue, but does not size Vera's realized share.\nJ2b 1 3 The management target implies more than $40 billion, but I discount it to roughly $38–39 billion because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand and the AMD comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk. The market-growth, deployment, and evaluation evidence supports demand but does not quantify NVIDIA's share enough to offset that discount.\nJ2b 2 3 I imply approximately $38–39 billion for FY2028 Vera CPU revenue. The more-than-$40-billion management expectation is not backed here by Vera shipment volumes in hand, so I discount it for execution risk; the competitive benchmarks add risk but do not quantify a revenue adjustment.\nJ2b 3 3 Approximately $38–39 billion. I discount the more-than-$40-billion management expectation because the evidence gives no Vera shipment volumes in hand; broad expected deployment supports substantial sales, while AMD's reported performance comparisons add execution risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction.\nT-old-1-Q28\nv7 1 3 Roughly $60–65 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report. The $43 billion company-based figure, later announced investments, and the approximately $70 billion Bank of America estimate point above $60 billion, discounted for overlap, funding before the report, and the estimate’s unspecified measurement date.\nv7 2 3 Approximately $60–65 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report. This starts from the $25 billion reported commitment balance and the $18 billion FY2027 earmark, then gives weight to BofA’s approximately $70 billion equity-commitment estimate and the more than $40 billion committed to equity investments in 2026, discounted for overlap and amounts funded before the reporting date.\nv7 3 3 Approximately $60–70 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report, with a midpoint near $65 billion. This carries forward the roughly $70 billion Bank of America estimate as an upper anchor, but discounts it for its unspecified measurement date and for commitments that may be funded before the report; the company-based $43 billion figure anchors the lower end.\nJ2a 1 2 I estimate roughly $50 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report, with a reasonable range of about $45–55 billion. The company’s $25 billion reported balance and remainder-of-year plans anchor the estimate; later named investments and the reported 2026 commitment pace add upside, but I discount the $70 billion third-party estimate because its date and treatment of funded versus outstanding commitments are unclear.\nJ2a 2 3 About $65 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report. The roughly $70 billion Bank of America estimate and more than $40 billion committed during 2026 point above the company-based reference, but their timing and how much remains outstanding are unclear, so I discount them rather than add gross deal amounts.\nJ2a 3 3 About $65 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report. The $43 billion company-based figure is the floor, while Bank of America’s approximately $70 billion estimate and the reported pace of 2026 commitments support a higher balance; I discount those figures for uncertain measurement dates and for investments that may be funded before the report.\nJ2b 1 3 I estimate about $70 billion of committed equity-investment obligations outstanding at the FY2027 annual report, in rung 3. Bank of America’s roughly $70 billion estimate is a broad estimate of equity investments committed, not a dated balance of obligations still outstanding; I use it as an anchor, tempered by the company’s $25 billion July commitment balance and $18 billion rest-of-year figure, and do not add later deal amounts or the 2026 commitment flow mechanically because they may overlap \nJ2b 2 3 I estimate roughly $65 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report, within a $60–70 billion range. The estimate is anchored by Bank of America's approximately $70 billion equity-commitment estimate, tempered by the company's lower disclosed commitment figures and uncertainty about which announced investments will still be unfulfilled obligations at the report date.\nJ2b 3 3 About $65–70 billion of outstanding commitments at the FY2027 report, with roughly $68 billion as a central estimate. I use Bank of America’s approximately $70 billion estimate as a broad exposure proxy, temper it for the company’s lower, specifically reported $25 billion balance and the possibility that some commitments will be funded before year-end, and treat the later named deals as support for continued commitments rather than adding their gross amounts mechanically.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"**The carried answer is a plausible influence. The fork cannot isolate it.**\n\n- **Anchor.** I checked old-3 Q3, old-2 Q29 and old-2 Q6. Each final request repeats the disputed calculation. Across all 24, 21 carry the rejected range, two carry a different range and one has no prior answer. The stored runs held the final range for 1–46 judgements, median 8.5, including the fork. [forkcases.json:270](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/forkcases.json:270)\n- **Reading.** Defer the 432 cards only if Result 1 stays undecided. Rule (a) counts supported reasoning, so nine same-range repairs could get J2b to 12. Rule (b) also needs reading because an unchanged range can lose support. Four reviewed cases establish neither result. [DESIGN-J2.md:125](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:125)\n- **Fork.** Eighteen forks show different evidence from the final request. Q29’s earlier answer already adds $25B and $18B. The first use of a range need not be the first error. Twelve recurrences and eight fewer are screening cutoffs. Eleven cannot establish bad luck, and eight moves can land on equally unsupported answers. To test the anchor, pair identical final requests with and without the previous conclusion, flag hidden evidence, and blind-read against the actual request. The current deck builder uses final-request cards. [judge_probe.py:322](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:322) · [judge_probe.py:623](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:623)\n- **Slice.** Yes. Compare v7 and the candidate from July 1 with old links fixed. Let each judge build its own conclusions, citations, theme effects and queue. That tests whether mistakes accumulate before September 6. [PLAN.md:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md:63)\n\nDISAGREE: Test the previous conclusion on identical evidence before attributing the result to anchoring, and read the final deck before declaring either arm passed or failed."}}
